WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.060
Hi, everyone. Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 36.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:10.800
It's May 12, 2026. Some awesome AI developments today.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.040
And we're going to make all of it make sense. Let's get into it.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.860
So imagine you are texting a friend about a homework idea.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:23.260
But every time you send a message, you have to wait ages for them to type back.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.900
That back and forth feels slow and choppy, right?

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:33.880
Today's big story is about a company called Thinking Machines that wants to fix exactly that problem with AI.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.720
They are building an AI that listens to your voice and starts thinking about its answer,

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:43.500
while you are still speaking instead of waiting until you finish.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:48.440
Think of it like switching from sending voice notes back and forth

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:52.660
to having a real phone call where both people can jump in naturally.

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:58.800
The system hears your words as they arrive, just like your ears catch sound right away

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.540
and it begins shaping a reply in the background.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:06.640
This matters because most AI today feels stiff and one-sided,

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:11.680
especially when you are brainstorming school projects or just chatting about your day.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:18.020
If it works, future homework helpers or creative tools could flow like talking to a friend

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:21.780
who gets you in real time, without constant pauses.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:29.240
For you personally, that means smoother conversations when you need quick ideas for an essay or game design

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:31.880
instead of typing and waiting repeatedly.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:36.440
The company is still building it, so there is no public demo yet.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.020
But you can follow updates on tech news sites that cover new AI experiments.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:47.200
Keep an eye out for early tests that might appear once they are ready for people to try.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:55.340
OK, now for my favorite part of the show, The Deep Dive, where we look under the hood.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:00.980
You know how when you are texting, your phone shows three dots while your friend types

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:02.900
and you just have to wait.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:09.400
Now picture instead that your friend could start replying out loud the moment you begin speaking,

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:12.040
even before you finish your sentence.

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:16.580
That is the idea behind this real-time AI approach.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:19.720
And here is how it actually works step by step.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:26.820
First, the system hears your words as they come in, exactly like your ears pick up sound.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.340
The instant it arrives.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:33.500
Second, it starts figuring out what you are asking while you are still talking,

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.420
instead of hitting pause until you hit send.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:41.940
Third, it begins shaping its answer in the background so it can reply smoothly

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:44.320
without a big delay once you stop.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:50.960
Think of it like a relay race where the next runner starts moving the second the baton is handed off,

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:53.620
rather than waiting at the finish line.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:57.720
And that is basically what thinking machines is trying to build,

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:03.220
an AI that treats talking like a live conversation instead of a turn-based game.

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:12.100
So next time someone says real-time AI dialogue, you can tell them it is basically removing the waiting game from chats.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.920
Not so scary, right?

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:22.820
Now let's look at some cool stuff you can try today, starting with something free and relaxing.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:29.780
Anthropic just turned their Constitution document into an audiobook you can listen to at no cost.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:39.720
This document is basically a set of rules that helps their AI name Claude stay helpful, honest, and safe instead of giving harmful answers.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:49.300
It is read by two of the people who wrote it, and it includes a question and answer section about how they decided what the AI should and should not do.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:57.960
Think of the Constitution like a rule book for a game that keeps everyone playing fair and safe, so the AI does not wander into trouble.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:08.640
If you have ever wondered why AI sometimes refuse certain requests, this is a chill way to understand the thinking behind safe for AI without reading anything.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:19.100
Go to the Anthropic website on your phone or laptop, find the Constitution audio, press play, and listen while you are walking or doing homework.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:26.280
Next up is a handy new way to find good AI stories without getting lost and random posts.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:38.120
Dig the old school site that used to show the Internet's best links is back and now focused on collecting the most important AI updates in one place.

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:46.980
Instead of scrolling through dozens of random feeds, it pulls together stories from key voices so you see only the stuff worth your time.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:54.960
This is perfect if you want to stay up on new tools without getting buried in tech jargon that feels like reading a foreign language.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:05:04.380
Head to the Dig website on your browser, sign up for their beta if it is open, and browse the AI section to see what stories they are highlighting.

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:13.600
Try picking one headline that sounds interesting and clicking through to read the original article like a treasure map leading you to the good stuff.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:17.660
Here are two quick bits to round things out.

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:29.260
This Saturday at 6 in the evening, Eastern Time, a group called AI Saturdays is hosting a free virtual meetup that walks beginners through setting up a language model on their own computer.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:37.180
Note coding experiences need it for the basics, and they show you the steps live so you can follow along from your laptop.

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:47.740
Open AI also launched a tool called Daybreak that uses their models to find and fix security issues and software before problems get worse.

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:57.620
It is aimed at teams that protect websites and apps, but it shows how AI is moving from just answering questions to actively keeping things safe.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:05.000
That's it for today. Remember, every AI expert started exactly where you are right now.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:11.560
If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it. That's literally how learning works.

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:15.740
Stay curious, keep experimenting, and we'll see you tomorrow.

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:22.700
And if you'd rather watch, then listen, find us on YouTube at Narra Network, links in the show notes.

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:30.280
This episode used AI voice synthesis of my voice. Editorial selection and analysis are my own.
